.TH CTPOOL_SUBMIT_FUTURE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_submit_future \- submit a task that returns a void* result (blocking)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_future *ctpool_submit_future(ctpool pool,
                                    void *(*fn)(void *),
                                    void *arg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a future object and submits a task to the pool. The task
calls fn(arg) on a worker thread. It then stores the void* pointer that
fn returned in the future, so that ctpool_future_get can read it
later.

The future that comes back has a reference count of 2 on success. The
caller holds one reference. The task in the queue holds the other
inside the library. Each ctpool_future_get call that runs on the future
adds one more. The caller must call ctpool_future_free exactly one time
when it is done.

For a bounded queue this function blocks until the queue has a free
slot. Use ctpool_try_submit_future or ctpool_timed_submit_future when
you do not want a block.

The call returns NULL, and does not wait, in one case. That case is a
call from a task that runs on this same pool, while a bounded queue is
already at capacity. A call from the on_complete callback of that task
is the same case. The reason is the one that ctpool_submit(3) gives.
Only the workers of this pool can free a slot, and the caller is one of
them. ctpool_try_submit_future(3) and ctpool_timed_submit_future(3)
report that case as ccol_container_full. An unbounded queue never
reaches it at all.

A future always uses the standard malloc and free. It does not use a
custom allocator that the caller set at pool creation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool  Thread pool handle
fn    Task function returning void* (must not be NULL)
arg   Passed to fn (may be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool_future handle on success.
Returns NULL in these five cases:
  pool is CTPOOL_INVALID, or a stale and already destroyed handle
  fn is NULL
  the pool is in shutdown
  a bounded queue is at capacity and the caller is a worker of this
    same pool
  a memory allocation fails
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_try_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_timed_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_future_get (3),
.BR ctpool_future_done (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
